{"result": {"response": {"id": "gen-1787751108-aDDS7s5UMyV6IHstyalr", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Power over Ethernet (PoE) and ANSI/TIA Cabling Standards\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community is centered around Power over Ethernet (PoE) technology and its integration with various ANSI/TIA cabling standards, primarily ANSI/TIA 568-D. PoE enables Ethernet cables to transmit electrical power to devices, reducing the need for separate power supplies and power outlets. The ANSI/TIA 568-D standard specifically enhances support for high-power PoE and introduces configurations like Modular Plug Terminated Links (MPTL). Various network devices, including Access Points, IP Cameras, IP Phones, and IoT Devices, are key beneficiaries and users of PoE. The standards also address cable categories, with newer categories and specific considerations for patch cords impacting power delivery and performance.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Power over Ethernet (PoE) as a foundational technology\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Power over Ethernet (PoE) is a technology that allows Ethernet network cables to transmit electrical power to devices, thereby eliminating the need for separate power supplies. This capability significantly simplifies network installations and reduces clutter by consolidating data and power transmission over a single cable. The development of newer standards, such as the D series, explicitly considers and enhances support for high-power PoE applications. The broad applicability of PoE is evident in its use for a wide range of network devices. [Data: Entities (822, 890), Relationships (1122, 1169, 1191)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"ANSI/TIA 568-D as the leading standard for modern cabling\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The ANSI/TIA 568-D standard represents a significant evolution in structured cabling guidelines, building upon previous revisions like the ANSI/TIA 568-C series. This updated version reorganizes existing standards and introduces requirements crucial for modern networking demands. A key focus of ANSI/TIA 568-D is its enhanced support for high-power Power over Ethernet (PoE) and the introduction of new configurations like the Modular Plug Terminated Link (MPTL). This standard ensures the future support and longevity of cabling infrastructures by incorporating these advancements. [Data: Entities (821, 855), Relationships (1098, 1145, 1121, 1147)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Modular Plug Terminated Link (MPTL) configuration\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The Modular Plug Terminated Link (MPTL) configuration is a notable advancement supported by standards like ANSI/TIA 568-D. This configuration involves terminating the horizontal cable directly with an RJ45 connector at the terminal device, which is particularly suitable for fixed equipment such as Access Points and surveillance cameras. The MPTL configuration offers a streamlined approach to device connectivity within structured cabling systems, moving away from traditional wall plates and jacks. Its inclusion in updated standards reflects the evolving needs for flexible and direct device connections. [Data: Entities (826, 854), Relationships (1106, 1143, 1171, 1172, 1173, 1188, 1200)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Broad range of network devices powered by PoE\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Power over Ethernet (PoE) technology is utilized to power a diverse array of network devices, significantly simplifying infrastructure requirements. These devices include Access Points (APs), IP Cameras (CCTV), IP Phones, and Internet of Things (IoT) devices. By receiving both data and power over Ethernet cables, these devices reduce the need for local power outlets, contributing to cleaner installations and lower electrical infrastructure costs. The widespread adoption of PoE across these devices highlights its utility and importance in modern network deployments. [Data: Entities (839, 840, 841, 842, 864, 866, 864, 873), Relationships (1123, 1124, 1125, 1126, 1168, 1166)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Influence of Cable Categories and Standards on PoE Performance\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The performance and limitations of Power over Ethernet (PoE) are significantly influenced by the type of cable category used and the governing standards. Different cable categories, such as Category 3, 5e, 6, 6A, and the more recent Category 8, are designed to support specific transmission frequencies and power levels. Standards like ANSI/TIA 568-C.2 detail these categories, while ANSI/TIA 568-D and its sub-parts like ANSI/TIA 568.2-D provide updated guidelines that incorporate support for higher power PoE and consider factors like patch cord types (e.g., 28 AWG patch cords) which can introduce potential issues such as increased signal attenuation and electrical resistance. The network cable category directly impacts power limitations in PoE systems. [Data: Entities (836, 843, 831, 892), Relationships (1127, 1139, 1159, 1204, 1205, 1206, 1170, 1151, 1152)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Cost savings and infrastructure benefits of PoE\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The implementation of Power over Ethernet (PoE) technology offers significant cost savings and simplifies electrical infrastructure. By delivering power over the same Ethernet cables that carry data, PoE reduces the need for numerous power outlets and associated electrical wiring. This leads to lower installation costs and a more efficient use of electrical infrastructure. Consequently, the reliance on traditional power outlets is diminished in areas where PoE is deployed. [Data: Entities (865, 866), Relationships (1168, 1148, 1167)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Considerations for high-power applications and potential risks\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The evolution of standards like ANSI/TIA 568-D explicitly addresses high-power applications supported by PoE. However, there are potential disadvantages associated with PoE, including heat generation in cables, power limitations dictated by cable category and standards, and the risk of damage to devices that are not compatible with PoE. Therefore, careful consideration of compatible devices and adherence to updated standards are crucial to mitigate these risks and ensure safe and efficient power delivery. [Data: Entities (858, 859, 860, 885), Relationships (1149, 1151, 1153, 1154, 1175)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 6.5,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderately high due to the widespread adoption of PoE technology in critical network infrastructure and the potential risks associated with power delivery standards and device compatibility.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null, "reasoning": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "STOP"}}], "created": 1787751108, "model": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1557, "prompt_tokens": 5953, "total_tokens": 7510, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "image_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 5745, "video_tokens": 0}, "cost": 0.00070105, "is_byok": false, "cost_details": {"upstream_inference_cost": 0.00070105, "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 7.825e-05, "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 0.0006228}}, "formatted_response": null, "provider": "Google", "content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Power over Ethernet (PoE) and ANSI/TIA Cabling Standards\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community is centered around Power over Ethernet (PoE) technology and its integra